CAT = "pkpnmcCritical"; SEG = "CRI"
Q = [
("Which of these is one of the 4 Hs, the reversible causes of cardiac arrest?",
 "Hypovolaemia", ["Hypertension", "Hyperglycaemia", "Hyperthyroidism"],
 "The 4 Hs are hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hypo- or hyperkalaemia and other metabolic causes, and hypothermia. The team looks for and treats them during resuscitation."),
("An adult in cardiac arrest has asystole. Which drug is given as soon as IV access is in place?",
 "Adrenaline 1 mg IV", ["Atropine 3 mg IV", "Amiodarone 300 mg IV", "Calcium gluconate IV"],
 "In a non-shockable rhythm, adrenaline 1 mg IV is given as soon as possible and repeated every 3 to 5 minutes, with high-quality CPR between rhythm checks."),
("An adult in cardiac arrest now has an advanced airway in place. How are breaths given?",
 "About 10 a minute", ["2 breaths after every 30", "1 breath after every 5", "Only when the chest stops"],
 "With an advanced airway, compressions continue without pause and one breath is given about every 6 seconds. Over-ventilation is avoided."),
("Which heart rhythms can an automated external defibrillator shock?",
 "VF and pulseless VT", ["Asystole and PEA", "Sinus bradycardia", "Atrial flutter with a pulse"],
 "Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia are shockable. Asystole and pulseless electrical activity are treated with CPR and adrenaline, not a shock."),
("A motorcyclist is unconscious after a crash and may have a neck injury. How is his airway opened?",
 "Jaw thrust", ["Head tilt and chin lift", "Turning the head to one side", "Placing a pillow under the head"],
 "A jaw thrust lifts the tongue off the back of the throat with little movement of the neck. Head tilt is used only if the jaw thrust fails to open the airway."),
("A head-injured patient's Glasgow Coma Scale score falls to 8. Why is this a concern?",
 "The airway may need protecting", ["It is a normal score after sleep", "It shows the patient is fully alert", "It means the pupils are equal"],
 "A score of 8 or less means a deep coma, in which the patient may not keep the airway clear. Senior help is called, as intubation is often needed."),
("An adult has anaphylaxis after an injection. What is the first drug and route?",
 "Intramuscular adrenaline", ["Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV", "Chlorphenamine orally", "Salbutamol nebulised"],
 "Intramuscular adrenaline (1 mg/mL) into the outer thigh is the first treatment, given as soon as anaphylaxis is suspected and repeated after 5 to 15 minutes if needed. Antihistamines and steroids are not first-line."),
("A patient bleeding heavily after an injury is pale with a fast pulse and low blood pressure. How is he positioned while help comes?",
 "Flat, with legs raised", ["Sitting upright in a chair", "On the side with legs bent", "Head raised on two pillows"],
 "In hypovolaemic shock the patient lies flat with the legs raised, unless injuries rule it out. Bleeding is controlled by direct pressure."),
("There is continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber of a chest drain. What does this suggest?",
 "An air leak in the system", ["The drain is working normally", "The lung has fully healed", "The bottle needs emptying"],
 "Some bubbling on coughing is expected with a pneumothorax, but constant bubbling suggests air entering at a loose connection or from the lung. The tubing is checked and the doctor told."),
("A patient with chest trauma has severe breathlessness, absent breath sounds on one side and a trachea pushed to the other side. What is suspected?",
 "Tension pneumothorax", ["Simple bruising of the ribs", "Asthma attack", "Pleurisy from a chest infection"],
 "Air trapped under pressure pushes the mediastinum and trachea away from the affected side. It is treated at once by needle decompression, then a chest drain."),
("After days of vomiting, a patient's arterial gas shows pH 7.50, PaCO2 44 mmHg and bicarbonate 32 mmol/L. What does this show?",
 "Metabolic alkalosis", ["Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory acidosis", "Respiratory alkalosis"],
 "A high pH with high bicarbonate and a normal carbon dioxide level is metabolic alkalosis. Vomiting loses stomach acid, and fluids with potassium are usually needed."),
("At a mass casualty scene using colour triage, what does a red tag mean?",
 "Immediate treatment needed", ["Walking wounded, minor injury", "Dead or expected to die", "Can wait several hours"],
 "Red is the most urgent group: life-threatening problems that can be treated. Yellow can wait a short time, green are walking wounded and black are dead or expectant."),
("A farm worker has pinpoint pupils, heavy sweating, drooling and a slow pulse after spraying pesticide. Which antidote is given?",
 "Atropine", ["Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "Vitamin K"],
 "Organophosphate pesticides block cholinesterase. Atropine is given in repeated doses until the chest is dry and the pulse rises. Contaminated clothes are removed and the skin washed."),
("How is a patient with raised intracranial pressure usually positioned?",
 "Head up 30 degrees, neck in line", ["Flat, head turned well to one side", "Head down, legs raised", "Prone with neck flexed"],
 "Raising the head about 30 degrees with the neck in line helps blood drain from the brain. Flexing or turning the neck can block venous return and raise the pressure."),
("When is suction applied while suctioning an adult's airway?",
 "Only while withdrawing the catheter", ["Only while inserting the catheter", "Throughout both insertion and removal", "Before the catheter goes in"],
 "The catheter is passed in without suction and suction is applied as it is drawn back, with a gentle rotating movement, to reduce damage to the airway lining."),
("Which measure helps prevent pneumonia in a patient on a ventilator?",
 "Head of bed up 30 to 45 degrees", ["Lying flat at all times", "Changing the tubing every hour", "Stopping all mouth care for a day"],
 "Raising the head of the bed reduces aspiration of stomach contents. Regular mouth care and daily checks on readiness to wean are also part of the bundle."),
]
